{"units": [{"id": "dynastes-quote2", "translation": "O much lamented by your loved ones in death"}, {"id": "dynastes-quote3", "translation": "Why these mighty, mighty things?"}, {"id": "dynastes-quote4", "translation": "the bright lords who bring winter and summer to mortals, conspicuous in the sky."}, {"id": "dynatos-orth1", "translation": "able, capable"}, {"id": "dynatos-def1", "translation": "able, capable"}, {"id": "dynatos-quote1", "translation": "of these things you may say what is both possible and lawful to praise."}, {"id": "dyno-orth1", "translation": "to set, sink (of the sun)"}, {"id": "dyno-def1", "translation": "to set, sink (of the sun)"}, {"id": "dyno-quote1", "translation": "toward the setting sun."}, {"id": "duo-orth1", "translation": "two"}, {"id": "duo-def1", "translation": "two"}, {"id": "duo-note1", "translation": "For thus I corrected what is written in M as λήμασι δισσούς [with twofold purposes]."}, {"id": "duo-note2", "translation": "where it is uncertain whether he wrote μορφάς [forms] or μορφά [form]."}, {"id": "duo-note3", "translation": "There are many examples of the genitive and dative dual δυοῖν [of two], constantly joined with a dual noun, except for two places where Elmsley rightly restored the dual."}, {"id": "duo-note4", "translation": "(thus Elmsley for οἰμώγμασιν [lamentations])."}, {"id": "duo-note5", "translation": "(thus Elmsley for μιασμάτων [pollutions])."}, {"id": "duo-quote1", "translation": "two men."}, {"id": "duo-quote2", "translation": "two well-robed women."}, {"id": "duo-quote3", "translation": "seeing two Atreidae equal in spirit."}, {"id": "duo-quote4", "translation": "with twofold purposes"}, {"id": "duo-quote5", "translation": "these are two outlines of feet."}, {"id": "duo-quote6", "translation": "two dice and four."}, {"id": "duo-quote7", "translation": "for two forms will appear,"}, {"id": "duo-quote8", "translation": "forms"}, {"id": "duo-quote9", "translation": "form"}, {"id": "duo-quote10", "translation": "of two"}, {"id": "duo-quote11", "translation": "I will present you with one of two accounts."}, {"id": "duo-quote12", "translation": "he will wish for one of two things."}, {"id": "duo-quote13", "translation": "of these two lords."}, {"id": "duo-quote14", "translation": "the deity ceased after prevailing over two."}, {"id": "duo-quote15", "translation": "there was a double front of two armies."}, {"id": "duo-quote16", "translation": "a bridge yoking two lands."}, {"id": "duo-quote17", "translation": "in two lamentations"}, {"id": "duo-quote18", "translation": "lamentations"}, {"id": "duo-quote19", "translation": "to be obedient to two women thus."}, {"id": "duo-quote20", "translation": "by two polluters."}, {"id": "duo-quote21", "translation": "easily cutting off the heads of two serpents."}, {"id": "duo-quote22", "translation": "when two are present, half the account is at hand."}, {"id": "duo-quote23", "translation": "for he had assaults of two pollutions"}, {"id": "duo-quote24", "translation": "pollutions"}, {"id": "duo-word1", "translation": "or"}, {"id": "duomai-orth1", "translation": "to go under, sink"}, {"id": "duomai-def1", "translation": "to go under, sink"}, {"id": "duomai-def2", "translation": "to set, sink (of the sun)"}, {"id": "duomai-quote1", "translation": "I went under"}, {"id": "duomai-quote2", "translation": "but when he entered the yoke-strap of necessity."}, {"id": "duomai-quote3", "translation": "and he went into the cave like a wild boar."}, {"id": "duomai-quote4", "translation": "the whole house did not sink."}, {"id": "duomai-quote5", "translation": "when life was setting in its rays."}, {"id": "duromai-orth1", "translation": "to lament, bewail"}, {"id": "duromai-def1", "translation": "to lament, bewail"}, {"id": "duromai-note1", "translation": "For the other form ὀδύρομαι [I lament] see its own place. The tragedians do not seem to have said δύρομαι [I lament] except when the meter required it."}, {"id": "duromai-quote1", "translation": "do not lament the present woes."}, {"id": "duromai-quote2", "translation": "divine woes, alas, alas, lamenting elders."}, {"id": "duromai-quote3", "translation": "I lament"}, {"id": "duromai-quote4", "translation": "I lament"}, {"id": "dysankomistos-orth1", "translation": "hard to bring back, irreparable"}, {"id": "dysankomistos-def1", "translation": "hard to bring back, irreparable"}, {"id": "dysankomistos-note1", "translation": "It is said for δυσανακόμιστον [hard to bring back], which adjective Plutarch used in Life of Romulus ch. 28."}, {"id": "dysankomistos-quote1", "translation": "a mother's blood on the ground, hard to bring back."}, {"id": "dysankomistos-quote2", "translation": "hard to bring back"}, {"id": "dysankritos-orth1", "translation": "hard to discern"}, {"id": "dysankritos-def1", "translation": "hard to discern"}, {"id": "dysankritos-note1", "translation": "The scholiast explains it by δυσδιάγνωστοι [hard to discern]."}, {"id": "dysankritos-quote1", "translation": "hard to discern"}, {"id": "dysankritos-quote2", "translation": "toils hard to discern."}, {"id": "dysankritos-quote3", "translation": "hard to discern"}, {"id": "dysadelphos-orth1", "translation": "having an unfortunate brother or brothers"}, {"id": "dysadelphos-def1", "translation": "having an unfortunate brother or brothers"}, {"id": "dysadelphos-note1", "translation": "of Antigone and Ismene, sisters of Eteocles and Polynices"}, {"id": "dysadelphos-quote1", "translation": "alas, most unfortunate in your brothers of all women."}, {"id": "dysaianes-orth1", "translation": "mournful, calamitous"}, {"id": "dysaianes-def1", "translation": "mournful, calamitous"}, {"id": "dysaianes-note1", "translation": "The scholiast explains it by δυσθρήνητον [much lamented], δυστυχή [unfortunate]."}, {"id": "dysaianes-quote1", "translation": "raise a mournful, calamitous cry for the Persians."}, {"id": "dysaianes-quote2", "translation": "much lamented"}, {"id": "dysaianes-quote3", "translation": "unfortunate"}, {"id": "dysaion-orth1", "translation": "unhappy"}, {"id": "dysaion-def1", "translation": "unhappy"}, {"id": "dysaion-note1", "translation": "I restored it for δυσδαίμων [ill-fated]"}, {"id": "dysaion-note2", "translation": "which the meter of the strophic verse requires"}, {"id": "dysaion-note3", "translation": "In M is δυσδαίμων σφὶν ἁ τεκοῦσα [ill-fated to them the mother], with ἰώ [alas] omitted."}, {"id": "dysaion-quote1", "translation": "ill-fated"}, {"id": "dysaion-quote2", "translation": "alas, unhappy to them the mother."}, {"id": "dysaion-quote3", "translation": "ill-fated to them the mother"}, {"id": "dysaion-quote4", "translation": "alas"}, {"id": "dysalges-orth1", "translation": "painful, grievous"}, {"id": "dysalges-def1", "translation": "painful, grievous"}, {"id": "dysalges-quote1", "translation": "uttering shrill sounds with painful fortune."}, {"id": "dysalotos-orth1", "translation": "hard to capture"}, {"id": "dysalotos-def1", "translation": "hard to capture"}, {"id": "dysalotos-quote1", "translation": "may someone take the hard-to-capture rule."}, {"id": "dysameria-orth1", "translation": "unlucky day, misfortune"}, {"id": "dysameria-def1", "translation": "unlucky day, misfortune"}, {"id": "dysameria-quote1", "translation": "unlucky day"}, {"id": "dysameria-quote2", "translation": "he sends the Sphinx, the presiding dog of unlucky days."}, {"id": "dysanor-orth1", "translation": "marriage with bad men"}, {"id": "dysanor-note1", "translation": "i.e. marriage with bad men."}, {"id": "dysanor-quote1", "translation": "a marriage with bad men,"}, {"id": "dysarestos-orth1", "translation": "hard to please"}, {"id": "dysarestos-def1", "translation": "hard to please"}, {"id": "dysarestos-note1", "translation": "of the implacable Furies"}]}